  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Define \\(x \\heartsuit y\\) to be \\(|x - y|\\) for all real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y.\\) Which of the following statements is not true?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x \\heartsuit y = y \\heartsuit x\\) for all \\(x\\) and\n\\(y\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(2(x \\heartsuit y) = (2x) \\heartsuit (2y)\\) for all \\(x\\) and\n\\(y\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(x \\heartsuit 0 = x\\) for all \\(x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x \\heartsuit x = 0\\) for all \\(x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(x \\heartsuit y \\gt 0\\) if \\(x \\ne y\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2003-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Statement (C) claims \\(x \\heartsuit 0 = x,\\) but \\(x \\heartsuit 0 = |x - 0| = |x|,\\) which fails for negative \\(x.\\) For example, \\(-1 \\heartsuit 0 = 1 \\ne -1.\\)\n\nThe remaining statements all follow directly from the properties of absolute value.\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Rewrite each statement using \\(x \\heartsuit y = |x-y|\\)",
        "Pay special attention when one input is \\(0\\) and the other is negative"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
